deep.reconstruction:
There is no mathematics on this folio. It is page 5 of a printed Italian (Turin) daily newspaper. The death register on the page dates it to the night of 4-5 July 1865. No Riemann handwriting is visible: no formulas, no computations, no marginal annotations. The only handwritten mark is an archival pencil foliation '98' in the right margin beside the 'Decessi' paragraph. There are also a couple of small specks or ink dots near the bottom centre, which carry no content.

The printed content is local and national news:
- the end of a description of a newly decorated hall honouring the House of Savoy (paintings, statues by Vela, Varni, Albertoni, Dini and Simonetta; designer cav. Ferri made Commendatore of the Order of St Maurice);
- a theatre notice (ballet 'La Zingara' at the Teatro Alfieri);
- the opening of a gymnastics school on 1 August;
- a found postal money order;
- two identified corpses (Levrone Francesco, Pinoli);
- the Turin death register for 4-5 July 1865;
- 'Notizie italiane': Franco-Italian intention to reopen negotiations with Rome, a customs circular on the cholera in Egypt, sale of state property of the former Kingdom of Naples, and royal decrees from the Gazzetta Ufficiale of 3 July.

The sheet's presence in the bundle R.AbelTheta is consistent with Riemann's stay in Italy in 1865, when he was in Pisa and travelling. Presumably the newspaper was kept with, or used as a wrapper or scrap for, his papers. It has no mathematical relation to the previous page, which deals with differential forms a_{i,j} and homogeneous f in four variables with a binomial count (n+1)(n+2)(n+3)/6 + ..., nor to the next page, which is page 6 of the same newspaper.
